Lisbon is a city you climb. Every neighbourhood sits on its own hill, every hill has a viewpoint at the top — a *miradouro* — and the honest way to plan a day is to pick a hill and stay on it until the light changes. Three days is enough for three hills and one escape.

## Day one — Alfama and the castle

Start at the cathedral and walk uphill without a map. Alfama survived the 1755 earthquake, which is why its streets make no sense: they were laid out by people, not planners. Aim for the castle by mid-morning, before the coaches, and come back down the far side to the Miradouro de Santa Luzia for the view you came for.

The tram 28 runs through all of this. Ride it once, early, and then walk; it is a beautiful thing that has become a queue.

## Day two — Belém and the river

The monuments are along the river, west of the centre, and they are a morning: the monastery first (book the timed entry; the queue without one is the whole morning), then the tower, then the pastry shop that everybody names and that is, annoyingly, worth it. The afternoon belongs to the river path back east, or to the MAAT if the weather turns.

## Day three — Sintra

A palace town in a forest on a hill, forty minutes away by train from Rossio, and the reason a "Lisbon" trip has four stops on the map.

<Leg from="lisbon" to="sintra" />

Go early, go to Pena Palace first because everybody else goes second, and walk *down* from it through the park rather than queueing for the bus. If you have a fourth day, the coast at Cascais is a train from the other side of the city and a different trip entirely.

## What to put in the expenses

Lisbon is cheap by the standards of the capitals north of it, and the things that cost are the timed tickets — the monastery, Pena, the castle. Put those in as a single "Sights" category on the day you buy them, not the day you visit, so the running total tells the truth about your card.
